1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:29,000
It's no secret that our society has a digital device problem. Ninety-two percent of Americans own a smartphone. Research has found that we are more connected than ever before and yet feel more isolated, more lonely, more depressed than ever before.

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:57,000
How's that possible? Social scientists call this a collective action problem. We all agree that these devices are having a detrimental impact on our mental health, and yet it feels virtually impossible to withdraw from these devices because they are so interconnected in our lives. So what can we do?

3
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:22,000
Good morning, church. Welcome to our final week of our digital detox series. This is obviously a bit different than a lot of our typical sermon series, but I hope you found it well worth the time. I wanted to invest some significant time in this really important issue because I think it's something that if we don't pay attention to, it will happen to us rather than us making decisions about it.

4
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:41,000
And so hopefully you found it valuable and challenging, and hopefully you're doing some things around your screen time. And like Jamie said earlier, next week we're going to be kicking off a highly anticipated series on the book of Revelation, kind of getting back into our normal preaching through books of the Bible.

5
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:54,000
So, if you deleted your news apps for the digital detox, I'm going to want you to re-download those, because we're going to be talking a lot about the news and when the tribulation is going to be happening.

6
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:09,000
And that was a joke. It was a really bad joke, and if you didn't get the joke, do not worry. Church culture stuff, if you didn't get it, it's okay.

7
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:29,000
And yeah, the book of Revelation has often been kind of misused and misinterpreted, and what we're going to look at over the next many weeks is that it was actually a book written to seven churches who were suffering persecution for being followers of Jesus, and the book was intended to encourage them to persevere.

8
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:55,000
And so even this morning as we prayed about the kind of rejoicing and weeping that's happening in the room, I have been just, my heart and mind has been changed by Revelation chapter 4 and 5, which gives us an image of what the throne room of God looks like right now, so that those of us who are suffering have a picture for what's ultimately real.

9
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:17,000
And what we see on the throne is a lamb as though it had been slain. And this is such an encouragement to those of us who are suffering because it tells us that the victory that we have, the hope that we have for the future, is rooted in a person who suffered the same sufferings that we did.

10
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:37,000
It's actually the way in which sin and death and the powers and principalities were conquered, not with overwhelming force, but with sacrificial love. And it was God's eternal and good plan that He Himself would suffer alongside of us.

11
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:47,000
And so even this Father's Day, I'm thinking about those of you that have lost your father. I'm thinking about Jesus as the suffering servant. And as far as we can tell in the Scriptures, He Himself lost His father.

12
00:03:47,000 --> 00:04:02,000
And the book of Revelation continually points us to Jesus as the Lamb who was slain, the one who endured the same sufferings that you and I endured, and yet came out the other side with resurrection power.

13
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:12,000
And that is our hope that we are clinging to, no matter what we're going through, that we have a suffering servant who sits on the throne.

14
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:20,000
So looking forward to that series, I hope you will join us and ignore my bad joke-making skills this morning, all right? Let's pray.

15
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:31,000
Father in heaven, we are just again grateful that we get to enter your throne room in worship, not just with the people in this room, but with the people across the country and across the world.

16
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:38,000
From every different denominational background and every different theological stream and tribe.

17
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:52,000
God, those of us who have bowed the knee to Jesus, enter your throne room alongside the angels and multitudes of heavenly hosts, declaring that you are holy, holy, holy.

18
00:04:52,000 --> 00:05:02,000
God, that is not a just trite thing we do a few times a year to encourage us and to make us feel better.

19
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:15,000
It is the eternal story. And it's so easy to lose sight of that story on this side of the apocalyptic curtain.

20
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:34,000
It's so hard to see the victory of the slain lamb in a world that's so broken and fallen. And so God, give us eyes to see and hope for a future. It's in Jesus' name I pray. Amen.

21
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:45,000
All right, well, today I have a big goal of putting a bow on the entire Digital Detox series with the hope of giving all of us really one big practical application.

