shortcut to abundant life. By seizing the definition of good and evil from God for ourselves, rather than trusting and obeying the God who created us, we are invited to trust and put our hope in our definition of good and evil to experience life.

34
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:10,000
And today, we intentionally wanted to end this series on Father's Day with an invitation to all of us, but especially to those of us who have spiritual leadership and influence in the lives of children.

35
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:20,000
I want us to really think intentionally about what we want our relationship and our kids' relationship with screens to be moving forward.

36
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:35,000
And as adults in the room, this decision should be made with intentionality. It shouldn't just be something that happens to us. Our screen habits should not be something that we just participate in, but we make active decisions about.

37
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:54,000
And so I think the most practical invitation I can give to each of us at the end of this series is to invite us to craft what I would call a rule of life around our screens or our devices, both for us as individuals and for our families, for the children that are under our care.

38
00:08:54,000 --> 00:09:06,000
And rule of life is a term that is in vogue in many Christian circles nowadays. We've talked about it a little bit in the Practicing the Way series, but it is not a new idea.

39
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:18,000
It traces back through the monastic movement to the desert fathers and mothers to the early church who developed it out of their Jewish influence, steeped in the Old Testament story.

40
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:33,000
This is not a new idea. And what a rule of life is, at its most basic point, it is just a set of intentional practices, rhythms, and relationships designed to help people abide in Christ and to be formed into His image.

41
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:52,000
And so what my hope is, is that we will just take a look at our lives and our practices and that we would create a set of regular practices and rhythms and restrictions for ourselves in order to abide in Christ and to be formed into His image.

42
00:09:52,000 --> 00:10:04,000
It's just really practical. I want to be careful that we avoid any type of legalism or like the word rule just comes with, if you grew up in a church where there were lots of rules, it comes with some baggage.

43
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:12,000
What I'm not telling you to do is to write down all of these rules and follow them strictly and perfectly forever, and if not, you're a terrible person.

44
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:26,000
What I'm saying is that we have a relationship with a good shepherd who wants us to hear and listen to his voice, and so what if we just created some rhythms and practices in our life that allowed his voice to be the primary voice we focus on?

45
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:34,000
There's a ton of freedom in this. There's a ton of, "Oh, no, my rule of life says I'm not supposed to watch TV today, but it's storming outside."

46
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:44,000
Okay, I will receive an invitation from the Father to watch TV when it's storming outside, right? Like, it's just like freedom, and it's an invitational thing.

47
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:55,000
And I would even argue that when we looked at the Garden of Eden last week, that we were looking at an invitation from God for Adam and Eve to live into their own rule of life.

48
00:10:55,000 --> 00:11:03,000
He gave them all of the good trees in the garden, and he invited them to walk with him in the cool of the evening and to enjoy all of his good creation.

49
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:12,000
And yet, he invited them to set one self-imposed boundary, right? He didn't stand in front of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil and keep them from eating it.

50
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:22,000
