00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:56,000
which leads us to the rhythms. And I don't know about you, but when I think about the moral law and the cultural practices,

162
00:22:56,000 --> 00:23:02,000
especially in the Old Testament, I tend to think about them in a very formal and rigid way, right?

163
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:07,000
You read through the Torah and you've got all of these like strict rules of when to show up to the temple,

164
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:15,000
of how to show up to the temple, of what you bring to the temple. There's these very strict and formal ideas around the cultural practices and moral law.

165
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:25,000
But if you read what Moses says here, he tends to, it's not that those things aren't true, but he goes a step further.

166
00:23:25,000 --> 00:23:30,000
And in this passage, he talks about cultural practices that are much more ordinary.

167
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:43,000
"You shall teach them diligently to your children. You shall talk about them when you sit in your house, when you walk by the way, when you lie down, when you rise, you shall bind them as a sign on your hand."

168
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:53,000
These are very daily, ordinary rhythms of life. And the reason he does this is because Moses, like most pastors,

169
00:23:54,000 --> 00:24:06,000
we know that a handful of communal religious and cultural practices will not be everything that God needs, God's people need to remember God's story and submit to his authority in their lives.

170
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:16,000
Like me, the priests only had spiritual influence over the people when they gathered to the temple, which in the grand scheme of things is a very small percentage of their lives.

171
00:24:17,000 --> 00:24:25,000
A majority of their lives was lived in the ordinary daily rhythms of working and playing and laughing and just surviving.

172
00:24:25,000 --> 00:24:33,000
And it's in those ordinary regular rhythms where they're not gathered together corporately as the people of God at the tabernacle or the temple.

173
00:24:33,000 --> 00:24:44,000
It's in those daily rhythms when they are most prone to lose sight of God's story and to be dragged away into the story of the culture around them.

174
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:53,000
And so Moses seems to extend the cultural practices to the ordinary moments of life in the household and community.

175
00:24:53,000 --> 00:24:58,000
He says, "Diligently and daily teach your children about God's love."

176
00:24:58,000 --> 00:25:05,000
Keep His commandments on your heart. Talk about them when you relax at your house, when you travel to soccer practice, before you go to bed, and when you wake up.

177
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:12,000
Moses is inviting the people of God to create a functional rule of life that shapes their ordinary and everyday habits.

178
00:25:13,000 --> 00:25:23,000
This, again, is my primary application point for our entire series, is that if we do not have regular practices and rhythms in our ordinary daily lives,

179
00:25:23,000 --> 00:25:31,000
then what will we do when we sit down to relax, when we travel, when we sit down to eat, before we go to bed, when we wake up?

180
00:25:31,000 --> 00:25:34,000
What will be written on the walls of our houses?

181
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:45,000
It's no coincidence to me that all of these times that are listed in Deuteronomy 6 are all times that oftentimes we just pick up our phones.

182
00:25:45,000 --> 00:25:55,000
These are times and places that most of us are staring at screens, filling our lives with the voices of thieves and robbers competing for our attention and our allegiance to Jesus.

183
00:25:55,000 --> 00:26:02,000
And this isn't primarily an individual rhythm, but a corporate one. He says, "Teach them to your children."

184
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:08,000
He doesn't say, "What do you do with your time?" He says, "What does the community do with your time?"

185
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:18,000
And parents, if we do not place boundaries around our children's devices, our ordinary lives will be consumed by them.

186
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:25,000
And it is not an easy task. Your children will fight you. And they will say, "But, my friends."

187
00:26:29,000 --> 00:26:34,000
But none of us would let our kids go to a playground beside a busy highway without a fence.

188
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:41,000
And so why are we comfortable doing the same thing while they sit in our living rooms on couches with endless content at their fingertips?

189
00:26:41,000 --> 00:26:47,000
And again, this isn't just a job for parents, but for grandparents and uncles and family friends.

190
00:26:47,000 --> 00:26:52,000
Raising children takes a village, and the nuclear family did not exist in the Old Testament.

191
00:26:54,000 --> 00:26:59,000
And so if we want to become a people, communally, who grow in love of God and love of neighbor,

192
00:26:59,000 --> 00:27:06,000
we all have to be intentional to rehearse the story of God and His goodness to the community around us.

193
00:27:06,000 --> 00:27:15,000
It won't just happen when we're under this roof. It has to happen as we go throughout our ordinary, everyday lives.

194
00:27:15,000 --> 00:27:19,000
And if we are not intentional, we will naturally drift towards a different story.

195
00:27:20,000 --> 00:27:30,000
The question isn't, will we have rhythms? The question is, will those rhythms be intentionally designed around God's story or accidentally designed around someone else's?

196
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:40,000
Which leads me to the third R of the day. We're cruising. The risk of forgetting.

197
00:27:40,000 --> 00:27:47,000
Moses ends kind of this part of his speech after giving them a reminder. You've got to talk about it everywhere you go.

198
00:27:47,000 --> 00:27:53,000
You've got to rehearse the story of God. He ends with a risk of what will happen if they don't do that.

199
00:27:53,000 --> 00:28:00,000
He says, "The Lord is going to bring you to the land that he promised your forefathers with great cities.

200
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:07,000
Life is going to be good and comfortable and abundant, but take care lest you forget the Lord who brought you out of the land of Egypt,

201
00:28:07,000 --> 00:28:15,000
out of the house of slavery. Take care, pay attention to the story that you are living into,

202
00:28:16,000 --> 00:28:22,000
because if you are not intentional to remind yourself of the story of God's goodness to bring you into

203
00:28:22,000 --> 00:28:27,000
abundant and eternal life, then you will live into a different story."

204
00:28:27,000 --> 00:28:34,000
He says, "People often begin to enjoy the gifts of God apart from the presence of God,

205
00:28:34,000 --> 00:28:40,000
and we slowly worship creation rather than the Creator. Our lives will be formed by a different story.

206
00:28:40,000 --> 00:28:45,000
It won't happen immediately or drastically. It does not happen overnight,

207
00:28:45,000 --> 00:28:51,000
but slowly over time, God's people will forget their story.

208
00:28:51,000 --> 00:28:55,000
We will find ourselves back at the tree of the knowledge of good and evil

209
00:28:55,000 --> 00:28:58,000
with that same choice that Adam and Eve had,

210
00:28:58,000 --> 00:29:02,000
and that same doubt in our mind that God is good because we haven't rehearsed the story.

211
00:29:02,000 --> 00:29:05,000
We haven't reminded ourselves of all that God has done for us.

212
00:29:05,000 --> 00:29:10,000
We haven't set our minds on things above, and we will find ourselves again

213
00:29:10,000 --> 00:29:14,000
or our children will find themselves again at the tree of the knowledge of good and evil

214
00:29:14,000 --> 00:29:16,000
with the same decision to make.

215
00:29:16,000 --> 00:29:20,000
Will I trust that God is good, and will I trust that his definition of wisdom is good,

216
00:29:20,000 --> 00:29:24,000
and it will lead me to abundant life, or will I seize that definition for myself?

217
00:29:24,000 --> 00:29:29,000
And if we have not rehearsed the story, if we have not reminded ourselves of God's goodness in our lives,

218
00:29:29,000 --> 00:29:35,000
the temptation is slowly over time, our lives will be built on a different story.

219
00:29:35,000 --> 00:29:38,000
We will reject God's wisdom for our own.

220
00:29:38,000 --> 00:29:43,000
We will choose what seems right in our own eyes.

221
00:29:43,000 --> 00:29:48,000
And unfortunately, this is exactly what happens to our original audience.

222
00:29:48,000 --> 00:29:53,000
They move into the promised land, they settle down into a new normal,

223
00:29:53,000 --> 00:29:57,000
and towards the end of their settling down into the new normal,

224
00:29:57,000 --> 00:30:02,000
Moses is kind of the next leader in line after Moses, Joshua,

225
00:30:02,000 --> 00:30:08,000
who's leading the people, who's reminding the people of the same thing that Moses is doing in Deuteronomy 6.

226
00:30:08,000 --> 00:30:12,000
He confronts the people with the same decision, and he makes a bold declaration

227
00:30:12,000 --> 00:30:17,000
that many of us are very familiar with, thanks to Hobby Lobby.

228
00:30:17,000 --> 00:30:23,000
You know where I'm going? Choose this day whom you will serve.

229
00:30:23,000 --> 00:30:29,000
Thank you, Hobby Lobby. But as for me and my house, we will serve the Lord.

230
00:30:29,000 --> 00:30:33,000
This is Joshua's bold declaration. One generation removed from Moses,

231
00:30:33,000 --> 00:30:36,000
reminding the people of the same thing. We're entering the promised land.

232
00:30:36,000 --> 00:30:41,000
We know what might happen. We might be tempted to forget, but me, Joshua, I, as your leader,

233
00:30:41,000 --> 00:30:44,000
I am choosing today that me and my household will serve the Lord.

234
00:30:44,000 --> 00:30:47,000
And all of the nation of Israel agrees with Joshua.

235
00:30:47,000 --> 00:30:57,000
Yes, we will serve the Lord. And then we read in Judges 2:7,

236
00:30:57,000 --> 00:31:02,000
"And the people served the Lord all the days of Joshua,

237
00:31:02,000 --> 00:31:05,000
and all the days of the elders who outlived Joshua,

238
00:31:05,000 --> 00:31:08,000
who had seen all the great work that the Lord had done for Israel.

239
00:31:08,000 --> 00:31:15,000
And Joshua, the son of Nun, the servant of the Lord, died at the age of 110 years."

240
00:31:15,000 --> 00:31:20,000
Lord, take me before that. Sorry.

241
00:31:20,000 --> 00:31:23,000
"Joshua died at the age of 110 years,

242
00:31:23,000 --> 00:31:26,000
and they buried him within the boundaries of his inheritance

243
00:31:26,000 --> 00:31:30,000
in the hill country of Ephraim, north of the mountains of Geash,

244
00:31:30,000 --> 00:31:33,000
and all the generations who were gathered to their fathers.

245
00:31:33,000 --> 00:31:38,000
And there arose another generation after them

246
00:31:38,000 --> 00:31:41,000
who did not know the Lord

247
00:31:41,000 --> 00:31:45,000
or the work that he had done for Israel."

248
00:31:45,000 --> 00:31:51,000
Joshua and the people of God, they make a bold declaration of surrendered obedience,

249
00:31:51,000 --> 00:31:57,000
but evidently, somewhere along the way, they lost sight of the regular rhythms

250
00:31:57,000 --> 00:31:59,000
of their families and their communities.

251
00:31:59,000 --> 00:32:02,000
They stopped rehearsing the story of God,

252
00:32:02,000 --> 00:32:05,000
and the consequence is that their children,

253
00:32:05,000 --> 00:32:09,000
the children of those who had just entered the promised land,

254
00:32:09,000 --> 00:32:15,000
on the power and might of God's visible victory,

255
00:32:15,000 --> 00:32:23,000
they didn't know the Lord or the things that he had done,

256
00:32:23,000 --> 00:32:27,000
which leads them right back to the tree of the knowledge of good and evil,

257
00:32:27,000 --> 00:32:31,000
and the defining attribute of this generation in the book of Judges

258
00:32:31,000 --> 00:32:34,000
summarizes this generation with this statement,

259
00:32:34,000 --> 00:32:39,000
"Everyone did what was right in their own eyes."

260
00:32:39,000 --> 00:32:44,000
So we've got a generation of people making a bold declaration that us, me, my household,

261
00:32:44,000 --> 00:32:47,000
we're going to serve the Lord in the next generation,

262
00:32:47,000 --> 00:32:50,000
and their children are back at the tree of the knowledge of good and evil

263
00:32:50,000 --> 00:32:51,000
with the same decision to make.

264
00:32:51,000 --> 00:32:54,000
Will we trust the Lord and his definition of goodness,

265
00:32:54,000 --> 00:32:57,000
or will we seize it for ourselves?

266
00:33:00,000 --> 00:33:07,000
And they don't know God or what he has done for them.

267
00:33:07,000 --> 00:33:13,000
And my fear is that if we are not intentional in this generation,

268
00:33:13,000 --> 00:33:16,000
mothers and fathers and grandmothers and aunts and uncles,

269
00:33:16,000 --> 00:33:21,000
if we are not intentional, not just to show up to church on Sunday every once in a while,

270
00:33:21,000 --> 00:33:23,000
not to just get plugged into a small group,

271
00:33:23,000 --> 00:33:29,000
but if we are not intentional to daily rehearse the story of God in our communities and in our families,

272
00:33:29,000 --> 00:33:37,000
our children might just forget the goodness of God.

273
00:33:37,000 --> 00:33:40,000
They might not even know him to begin with.

274
00:33:40,000 --> 00:33:43,000
They might just know that their parents know him.

275
00:33:43,000 --> 00:33:52,000
So what are we doing, church, with our ordinary daily rhythms?

276
00:33:52,000 --> 00:33:56,000
Are they saturated with us rehearsing?

277
00:33:56,000 --> 00:34:00,000
And I'm not talking about like strict, like you got to have morning Bible study.

278
00:34:00,000 --> 00:34:21,000
And I'm talking about like just as you're reading Deuteronomy 6, as you go along the way, as you travel, as things come up, as your kids ask you questions about creation, about every normal, ordinary thing, are you reminding them of how good God has been and of his invitation to surrender to his definition of good and evil?

279
00:34:21,000 --> 00:34:28,000
And are we modeling that type of surrender, or are we just telling our kids that God is good and you should surrender to him while we don't do that?

280
00:34:28,000 --> 00:34:38,000
Do we have intentional rhythms aimed at reminding us of God's wisdom and helping us to cultivate Eden-like circumstances?

281
00:34:38,000 --> 00:34:50,000
Or do we have unintentional rhythms captured by screens that are causing us to fall back into the trap of defining good and evil for ourselves?

282
00:34:50,000 --> 00:34:59,000
So my final challenge, my one application point, landing the plane with hopefully not too much turbulence, is to consider your daily and weekly rhythms.

283
00:34:59,000 --> 00:35:13,000
Consider your screen time and how it impacts and influences those rhythms. Dads and moms and grandparents and aunts and uncles and close friends, how are your screen times impacting your relationship with the next generation?

284
00:35:14,000 --> 00:35:22,000
What are we teaching them about life and abundance and goodness in the way that we interact with our screens?

285
00:35:22,000 --> 00:35:32,000
And what rhythms and boundaries are we placing in their lives around their screens so that they have space to hear from God, so they have space to remember God's goodness in their life?

286
00:35:32,000 --> 00:35:43,000
And what might a rule of life, again, not a legalistic hard and fast rule, but what might some practices and rhythms look like for you and for your family around screen times?

287
00:35:44,000 --> 00:35:52,000
I put a book recommendation in the bulletin, and we'll put it in the newsletter this Sunday. It's called "Habits of the Household."

288
00:35:52,000 --> 00:36:03,000
It's a phenomenal book for parents about this very topic. It's very practical. It's written by a dad with a full-time job who doesn't act like it's easy, which is always good, right?

289
00:36:03,000 --> 00:36:08,000
It's always good to have somebody say like, "Hey, this is what we do, and this is how it fails and how hard it is."

290
00:36:08,000 --> 00:36:14,000
It's a wonderful book, but specifically chapter four is on their habits and rhythms around screen time.

291
00:36:14,000 --> 00:36:24,000
I would recommend it highly to any of you who are wanting to look into this. Again, not legalistic rules, but rhythms that flow out of a relationship, all reinforced around the central commandment.

292
00:36:24,000 --> 00:36:34,000
There's rules and statutes. There's plural stuff all around, but it's all around the central commandment to love the Lord our God with all of our heart, soul, mind, and strength.

293
00:36:34,000 --> 00:36:54,000
And so as we kind of land the plane on this message, land the plane on this series, I wanted to share a video testimony with you of a person in our congregation who's kind of taking this idea of developing a rule of life for her screens and for her kind of daily rhythms and routines.

294
00:36:54,000 --> 00:36:57,000
So I'd love for you to check this out, and then I'll come back up and we'll end.

295
00:36:57,000 --> 00:37:04,000
The moment I caught myself mindlessly scrolling through Venmo is when I realized I might have a problem.

296
00:37:04,000 --> 00:37:12,000
Hey, my name's Carmen Lawyer, and my husband William and I have been coming to Canvas since August, so almost a year.

297
00:37:12,000 --> 00:37:20,000
The digital fast was something that really spoke to me, and I sent Ryan an email, and he was like, "Oh, can you talk about your story?"

298
00:37:20,000 --> 00:37:30,000
So, here I am. I used to have a 30-minute commute to work, and you can only listen to so much music, and so I started listening to podcasts.

299
00:37:30,000 --> 00:37:42,000
And through that time, I learned this concept of parenting your phone, being present. I was like, "Intriguing idea," but I didn't really feel convicted, necessarily.

300
00:37:42,000 --> 00:37:56,000
January, Passion 2020, and I finally realized, "Okay, I might have a problem." So, I gave my friend my phone, and she set a one-hour social media limit for me.

301
00:37:56,000 --> 00:38:09,000
The next day, my hour of social media time was gone by noon, and that was me trying to pace myself so I didn't run out of time.

302
00:38:09,000 --> 00:38:18,000
After that is when I opened Venmo, and without even intentionally doing it, and found myself scrolling.

303
00:38:18,000 --> 00:38:25,000
And that slightly terrified me a little bit, the fact that I was doing something that I didn't even realize I was doing.

304
00:38:25,000 --> 00:38:31,000
But I still didn't care enough. My heart wasn't behind it. I wasn't surrendered to the Lord.

305
00:38:31,000 --> 00:38:45,000
So fast forward to 2024, 2025, and I was teaching my students. I was trying to teach them for a 50-minute science class, and all of my kids had phones.

306
00:38:45,000 --> 00:39:01,000
One in particular comes to mind. She told my team of teachers and I regularly that she would go home, take a nap, wake up, eat dinner, and then stay up all night on her phone on social media.

307
00:39:01,000 --> 00:39:15,000
No wonder I was having a problem trying to engage her in my class. I continued this journey of trying to parent my phone, and in January 2025, I decided to delete all social media from my phone.

308
00:39:15,000 --> 00:39:22,000
I was trying to get serious about this. I saw what was happening with my kids, and I was like, "I don't want to be like that."

309
00:39:22,000 --> 00:39:38,000
In that time, I read like 13 books in the month of January, one of which was "The Anxious Generation." While I was reading that, I learned that average users receive a notification about every five minutes.

310
00:39:38,000 --> 00:39:47,000
Heavy users, much like myself or my students, more than likely, receive a notification about every minute.

311
00:39:47,000 --> 00:39:59,000
The next day at work, I put my phone in my desk drawer, and over several days, I started taking less and less work home.

312
00:39:59,000 --> 00:40:05,000
I was more focused during my planning periods and getting my work done at work. It was amazing.

313
00:40:05,000 --> 00:40:14,000
I wanted to find this balance of not being completely anti-technology, but also not letting it consume every bit of me.

314
00:40:14,000 --> 00:40:23,000
I found this app called BePresent, and my social media use dropped to less than 25 minutes per day.

315
00:40:23,000 --> 00:40:33,000
Ironically, I found the app on social media. But what was the terrifying thing was I didn't need a passcode or someone holding me accountable,

316
00:40:33,000 --> 00:40:43,000
because they used the same methodology to keep you off of your phone that the big tech companies used to keep you on it.

317
00:40:43,000 --> 00:40:49,000
I kind of carried around with that journey, but I still didn't love how much I was still being on my phone.

318
00:40:49,000 --> 00:40:56,000
So I deleted all my social media from my phone this past January, and I decided to not put it back on my phone.

319
00:40:56,000 --> 00:41:05,000
I felt so much freedom in that, and so I decided to have all my social media only on a Wi-Fi iPad.

320
00:41:05,000 --> 00:41:12,000
I now believe I have a lot more healthy boundaries with technology.

321
00:41:12,000 --> 00:41:18,000
Because Meta emails me on a regular basis about all I'm missing out on, and I'm like, "Okay."

322
00:41:18,000 --> 00:41:28,000
Instead, I do things like read books now. I love doing puzzles. I learn piano. I'm learning piano. I'm learning guitar.

323
00:41:28,000 --> 00:41:31,000
I have more presence in my everyday life.

324
00:41:31,000 --> 00:41:41,000
The most poignant example, I guess, that I can think of right now is I took my first solo trip to South Carolina back in March,

325
00:41:41,000 --> 00:41:47,000
and I stopped at a Chick-fil-A, and it was the first time that I was somewhere without someone else,

326
00:41:47,000 --> 00:41:53,000
and I didn't have the ability to scroll on my phone or my iPad,

327
00:41:53,000 --> 00:42:01,000
and I was sitting around and looking at the people around me and just enjoying being where I was.

328
00:42:01,000 --> 00:42:06,000
I was being very present in the moment, and ironically, I saw a lot of people on their phones,

329
00:42:06,000 --> 00:42:10,000
but I'm pretty sure I also saw someone on their first date, and I was like, "That's actually kind of cool."

330
00:42:10,000 --> 00:42:15,000
What God's taught me the most in this is it's really not about social media,

331
00:42:15,000 --> 00:42:19,000
but rather it's been about my attention and my heart all along.

332
00:42:19,000 --> 00:42:30,000
I was giving so much attention to my screen, and all that my attention wasn't going to God, my family, and all the people around me.

333
00:42:30,000 --> 00:42:39,000
I didn't just get my time back. I got my space back. I got space to pray, space to hear His voice, space to worship Him.

334
00:42:39,000 --> 00:42:47,000
Space to be present. And I'm not, like, anti-technology. I still, obviously, I have an iPad.

335
00:42:47,000 --> 00:42:57,000
But I've learned that anything competing for my attention can slowly take the place that God's meant to have, whether I realize it or not.

336
00:42:57,000 --> 00:43:05,000
This journey has been less about deleting the apps and more about learning how to steward my attention in a way that honors Him.

337
00:43:05,000 --> 00:43:13,000
And it's still very much a journey. I don't want to make this sound like I've mastered this, because I most definitely have not.

338
00:43:13,000 --> 00:43:23,000
Even after deleting social media from my phone, I still catch myself, to this day, searching for Facebook and my apps out of habit.

339
00:43:23,000 --> 00:43:31,000
It's not, again, it's not about perfection and arriving at this perfect moment, but it's being aware of where my attention is going

340
00:43:31,000 --> 00:43:36,000
and continually surrendering that and stewarding it well to what matters the most.

341
00:43:36,000 --> 00:43:42,000
Thank you for listening to this part of my story, and I hope you feel encouraged in some sort of way.

342
00:43:42,000 --> 00:43:54,000
Well, I hope that you were as encouraged by Carmen Story as I was. I love preaching.

343
00:43:54,000 --> 00:44:00,000
I love standing up here and encouraging with God's word, but there's not many better things than hearing stories from people

344
00:44:00,000 --> 00:44:07,000
who are putting God's word into practice. And one of my favorite themes from her story was her emphasis on how

345
00:44:07,000 --> 00:44:14,000
space away from screens allowed her to hear the voice of God. And that's really what this series is all about.

346
00:44:14,000 --> 00:44:21,000
That's what my heart has been throughout this series, is that we would just create enough space in our minds and our hearts

347
00:44:21,000 --> 00:44:29,000
so we could really hear the voice of God and what He's inviting us into, because the voice of God is always going to lead us to a more abundant life

348
00:44:29,000 --> 00:44:35,000
than the one we experience when we are not listening to it.

349
00:44:35,000 --> 00:44:42,000
So that theme of listening to the voice of God, it leads me into an important announcement that I need to make today.

350
00:44:42,000 --> 00:44:49,000
We had to make it today, so sorry about the awkward timing. But we made this announcement to our volunteers on Wednesday,

351
00:44:49,000 --> 00:44:58,000
and we wanted to share with the rest of you today that over the past few weeks, Pastor Michelle has been hearing from God about an

352
00:44:58,000 --> 00:45:06,000
opportunity to become the school counselor at Jordan Springs Elementary School in her neighborhood. This has been one of those

353
00:45:06,000 --> 00:45:13,000
hearing from God things that was challenging for her to hear, because she loves your kids and our kids so much.

354
00:45:13,000 --> 00:45:20,000
She loves our church so much. But she has discerned that the Lord is leading her to step down from her full-time role

355
00:45:20,000 --> 00:45:27,000
as children's pastor and calling her to a new ministry at the school. She is not leaving the church.

356
00:45:27,000 --> 00:45:34,000
She still plans on being involved in our kids' ministry, but obviously her level of involvement will change significantly

357
00:45:34,000 --> 00:45:42,000
as she steps into this new role. And those of us who have walked with Michelle over the past few weeks

358
00:45:42,000 --> 00:45:50,000
around this decision are very sad that her role is changing. We love her. We are so thankful for her.

359
00:45:50,000 --> 00:45:56,000
But we are also extremely excited for her and for the gospel impact that this will have on one of our local

360
00:45:56,000 --> 00:46:03,000
public schools. This is kingdom stuff. Our vision is to cultivate the kingdom of God in Winchester

361
00:46:03,000 --> 00:46:08,000
as it is in heaven, and that doesn't primarily happen under this roof. But it happens as each one of us

362
00:46:08,000 --> 00:46:15,000
take the good news of Jesus to where we live, work, and play. So not only are we excited for Michelle,

363
00:46:15,000 --> 00:46:25,000
but we are also expectant for us as a church because God brought us Michelle at the right time. She actually discerned her

364
00:46:25,000 --> 00:46:32,000
call to step into children's ministry in a service like this one, when a need for a children's

365
00:46:32,000 --> 00:46:38,000
director was announced on a Sunday morning about eight years ago at Canvas. So please, be in prayer for

366
00:46:38,000 --> 00:46:43,000
Michelle and for this new incredible ministry opportunity that she has. Be in prayer for our

367
00:46:43,000 --> 00:46:50,000
church and our leadership as we seek the Lord to figure out what this means for us moving forward. And I

368
00:46:50,000 --> 00:46:54,000
hope that this testimony of hearing from God's voice, both from Carmen and from

369
00:46:54,000 --> 00:47:00,000
Pastor Michelle, will encourage all of us and remind us of the importance of

370
00:47:00,000 --> 00:47:06,000
listening for God's voice in our everyday life. What might God be inviting you into?

371
00:47:06,000 --> 00:47:11,000
Maybe it's not a big career change. Maybe it's an unconfessed sin you need to

372
00:47:11,000 --> 00:47:17,000
address, forgiveness that you need to extend, a neighbor that you need to reach out to. I

373
00:47:17,000 --> 00:47:23,000
don't know what it is for you, but I know that God is often inviting us to partner with him in

374
00:47:23,000 --> 00:47:28,000
his work around the world. And if we don't listen, we might miss out on so many blessings

375
00:47:28,000 --> 00:47:36,000
that God wants to pour out through us and on us as we step into obedience to him.

376
00:47:36,000 --> 00:47:43,000
So I'm going to pray to end our gathering, and then I think what we need, a little bit different

377
00:47:43,000 --> 00:47:47,000
this morning, I think we need our kids, our parents to pick their kids up through this hallway

378
00:47:47,000 --> 00:47:53,000
because there's ice cream chaos happening out here. And it's just going to be a

379
00:47:53,000 --> 00:47:59,000
chaotic morning, and I'm sorry about that, but that's just the nature of buying an old fire hall

380
00:47:59,000 --> 00:48:05,000
and putting a church in it is there's lots of hallways and not a lot of big gathering spaces.

381
00:48:05,000 --> 00:48:09,000
So we're going to pick up our kids out here. There's an ice cream fundraiser in the lobby.

382
00:48:09,000 --> 00:48:15,000
We kind of opened up the office parking lot out on the front side of the building if you want to get ice cream

383
00:48:15,000 --> 00:48:21,000
and hang out and talk to people and fellowship. So please be doing that.

384
00:48:21,000 --> 00:48:31,000
And yeah, let's pray. Father, we are just grateful for another week,

385
00:48:31,000 --> 00:48:39,000
another opportunity to think about your voice in our lives, to think about our daily and regular rhythms.

386
00:48:39,000 --> 00:48:46,000
And are they centered around you? And are they reminding us of your story of deliverance and healing and salvation?

387
00:48:46,000 --> 00:48:56,000
Or are we living into a different story, a story of the culture around us that does not know you, and does not know the things that you have done?

388
00:48:56,000 --> 00:49:11,000
God, are we allowing you to define good and evil? Or are we seizing that decision for ourselves? God, may this theme, just may you bring it to our minds and our hearts this week by your Holy Spirit.

389
00:49:11,000 --> 00:49:23,000
Help us to create healthy rhythms and boundaries that allow us more space to hear from you. And God, we just trust that as your people step out into obedience to your invitations in their lives, that your kingdom will spread.

390
00:49:23,000 --> 00:49:36,000
That Eden-like conditions will go beyond just what happens in our families and in our churches, but to the world around us. God, do this for your glory, for our good, and for the good of our neighbors. In Jesus' name I pray. Amen.

391
00:49:40,000 --> 00:49:40,320
Church, peace.
